{"reference":"Mark 5","book":"Mark","chapter":5,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Ancient Accounts of Unclean Spirits","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The first episode is narrated within ancient understandings of hostile spirits and exorcism. It describes the man’s suffering and restoration in those terms; it should not be used to diagnose modern illness as demonic or to replace medical or mental-health care.","verse_refs":["Mark 5:1-20"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_mark_005_910ef45fa7990ae5a07cd5aa"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Details That Invite Careful Reading","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The Gerasene episode brings together a disputed location, the military-sounding name “Legion,” a large herd of pigs, liberation, fear, and the animals’ death. Readers have offered both historical and symbolic explanations for these details, but no single explanation is settled.","verse_refs":["Mark 5:1-20","Mark 5:21-43","Mark 5:41"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_mark_005_9abbc09661709e9513f91e59"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_3","text":"Mark places the woman’s healing inside Jairus’s urgent journey to his daughter, a recurring storytelling pattern in which one story is inserted into another so that the two episodes may interpret each other. Both women and girls are not merely background figures here: the woman acts as a petitioner and recipient of healing, while the child is restored. The preserved and translated words “Talitha cumi” also fit Mark’s practice of retaining several Semitic expressions.","verse_refs":["Mark 5:1-20","Mark 5:21-43","Mark 5:41"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_mark_005_9abbc09661709e9513f91e59"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}